WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.400
Chapter one treated each cluster as a candidate type.

2
00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:08.500
But can the same sensory neuron leave its cluster and later return?

3
00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:13.600
Follow its identity through development, injury and another species.

4
00:00:17.283 --> 00:00:22.603
Mouse sensory neurons begin subtype specialization after they have become neurons.

5
00:00:23.943 --> 00:00:26.943
Early cells coexpress several transcription factors.

6
00:00:28.163 --> 00:00:33.223
The cells are not blank; specialization here refers only to subtype identity.

7
00:00:34.583 --> 00:00:39.323
Over development, those teal badges narrow into subtype-restricted combinations.

8
00:00:40.583 --> 00:00:44.923
The broad starting population resolves into distinct blue branches.

9
00:00:46.067 --> 00:00:48.947
Signals from targets contribute to that narrowing.

10
00:00:50.347 --> 00:00:55.687
Removing nerve growth factor changed subtype-restricted expression in mouse dorsal

11
00:00:55.687 --> 00:01:01.887
root ganglia. Fewer distinct populations remained when nerve growth factor was

12
00:01:01.887 --> 00:01:08.687
absent. Keeping the affected neurons alive separated altered identity from selective

13
00:01:08.687 --> 00:01:13.647
death. The signal contributes; it does not set every fate alone.

14
00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:16.800
Now cut the peripheral axons.

15
00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:23.160
Several blue types lose their subtype markers and gather in one red Atf3-rich

16
00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:27.920
cluster. Is that cluster a new permanent type, or a shared state?

17
00:01:33.883 --> 00:01:36.163
It behaves as a shared injury state.

18
00:01:37.423 --> 00:01:40.123
Existing neurons from many subtypes enter it.

19
00:01:41.123 --> 00:01:45.523
The red program promotes regeneration while suppressing the usual subtype

20
00:01:45.523 --> 00:01:52.623
signatures. As axons regrow over time, subtype expression reappears and the neurons

21
00:01:52.623 --> 00:01:54.523
separate toward their earlier branches.

22
00:01:54.523 --> 00:02:00.103
Their fixed gold outlines reveal continuity beneath the changing RNA.

23
00:02:01.317 --> 00:02:06.617
The timing is associated with target reinnervation, but timing alone cannot prove

24
00:02:06.617 --> 00:02:11.497
cause. Tracing injured neurons argues against selective death.

25
00:02:12.697 --> 00:02:16.657
It still does not show that contact with the target restores identity.

26
00:02:18.017 --> 00:02:19.357
Keep the causal arrow dashed.

27
00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:22.340
Species adds another boundary.

28
00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:28.580
In mouse, Tafa4 is mainly associated with one blue C-fibre touch branch.

29
00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:34.860
In human tissue, TAFA4 was strongest in an Aδ touch population.

30
00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:40.200
It also appeared in cold-sensing and C-fibre touch populations.

31
00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:44.240
The same marker does not guarantee the same type.

32
00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:45.240
For more information visit www .fema4 .com

33
00:02:45.283 --> 00:02:48.863
A missing cross-species match may also come from method.

34
00:02:50.283 --> 00:02:52.703
Whole cells and nuclei capture different RNA.

35
00:02:53.983 --> 00:02:58.883
A missing group can reflect what the preparation captures, not biological absence.

36
00:03:00.303 --> 00:03:05.063
Integration methods are built to align data sets and can merge real differences.

37
00:03:06.683 --> 00:03:11.283
Match preparations, validate markers in tissue and seek independent function.

38
00:03:12.383 --> 00:03:16.123
An atlas is a snapshot of cells, methods and conditions.

39
00:03:17.623 --> 00:03:20.803
Follow cells through time before calling a cluster permanent.

40
00:03:22.283 --> 00:03:25.103
Cross species boundaries only after matching the view.

41
00:03:26.543 --> 00:03:29.743
Identity is biological, but every atlas samples it.
